{
 "id": "2024-xu-campus",
 "name": "Campus Landscape Types and Restoration",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original photographs: 12 photographs of four campus landscape types taken on three university campuses in Hefei, Anhui Province, China, three photos per type: green space (over 75% green coverage, large lawns and tall trees), blue space (open water with some plantings and waterfront facilities), gray space (hard-paved walks with seating and a few plantings) and movement space (varied sports facilities on lawn with a few trees). Participants give gender and age, look at the 12 photos grouped by type with the paper's description of each type (types in random order), choose the type of campus landscape they prefer to visit, say how often and how long they visit it, and then, with its photos still shown, rate that type on the paper's questionnaire, all on 5-point scales (1 = strongly disagree, 5 = strongly agree): 8 landscape-type perception items (peacefulness, naturalness, spatiality, sociability, after Grahn & Stigsdotter) and 12 items of the partial Perceived Restorativeness Scale (remoteness, extensibility, charming, compatibility). Last come 9 pro-social behaviour items (social reciprocity, public labor, social morality). Rebuilt from Xu, Wang, Wang, Tian, Zhang, Chen & Chen (2024), Campus landscape types and pro-social behavioral mediators in the psychological recovery of college students, Frontiers in Psychology 15: 1341990, with the photos cropped from its Table 1 and all items from its Table 3 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: green_1-3, blue_1-3, gray_1-3, movement_1-3; preferred_type records the chosen type and the ratings are keyed by it. Analysis as in the paper: ANOVA of perception and restoration scores across the chosen landscape types, Pearson correlations between the 11 dimensions, a structural equation model (perception -> restoration -> pro-social behaviour) and a PROCESS mediation test; the paper (264 college students, online) found 59.1% chose green space, restorative scores highest for green space (3.96) and then blue (3.79), gray (3.63) and movement space (3.56), and pro-social behaviour partially mediating the effect of landscape perception on restoration (41.5% of the total effect). Differences from the original study: (1) the paper does not say exactly how each participant's landscape type was set; its group sizes (Table 6) add up to the whole sample and its text reports which type students choose to visit, so here each participant rates the type they choose; the question wording for this choice, the visit frequency and duration options and the instruction texts are ours, because the paper does not print them; (2) the paper's questionnaire was on Wenjuanxing (Questionnaire Star) for college students in Anhui Province; here anyone can take part; (3) the age question is ours (the paper reports age groups and majors but not the questions); (4) the English item wording is the paper's Table 3 translation.",
 "author": "Xu, Wang, Wang, Tian, Zhang, Chen & Chen",
 "year": "2024",
 "category": "Academic Research",
